partition_odd_even should separate a list of integers into its even and odd members, keeping each group in its original relative order, and return the pair (evens, odds).

After creating the two empty lists, the function runs

        for x in lst:
            if x % 2 == 0:
                evens.append(x)
            else:
                odds.append(x)
        return (evens, odds)

Every element is examined once, in input order, and appended to exactly one of the two lists, so relative order within each group is preserved automatically and no element is lost or duplicated. The parity test x % 2 == 0 is the right one in Python for all integers, including negatives, because the modulo result takes the sign of the divisor: -3 % 2 is 1 and -8 % 2 is 0. Zero is even and goes to evens. The tuple is returned with evens first, the order the specification names, and an empty input yields ([], []).

To test a case the visible asserts do not cover, I traced [0, -3, 7, -8]. The loop sends 0 to evens, -3 to odds, 7 to odds and -8 to evens, so the function returns ([0, -8], [-3, 7]). That is correct: both lists keep the order in which their elements appeared, and the negative values are classified properly. The code contains no comparison with the example lists and no special handling tied to particular values, so the same single pass gives the right split for any list of integers of any length.

